{
  "summary": "Retains completed and echoed private broadcast transactions in the internal tracker and exposes them in the `getprivatebroadcastinfo` RPC output. The RPC returns a new `received_by_us` object detailing the peer address and timestamp for transactions echoed back from the network. When the private broadcast queue hits capacity, the oldest finished transaction is evicted to make room for new entries.",
  "problem": "Previously, transactions submitted via private broadcast were immediately dropped from tracking as soon as they were received back from the network or resolved, causing broadcast data to disappear instantly from `getprivatebroadcastinfo` and preventing operators from inspecting send history or reception confirmations.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'For finished transactions, this allows the inspection of when and to whom the transaction was sent as well as when/if it was echoed from the network.'",
    "doc/release-notes-36477.md: 'Transactions submitted via private broadcast are now retained after they are received back from the network. This allows inspecting the state of such transactions via the getprivatebroadcastinfo RPC. The information will now not vanish as soon as the transaction is echoed from the network.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "",
  "changed_since_previous": "Diff provided. Confirmed eviction semantics when reaching the transaction limit (evicting oldest finished transactions), recorded reception peer metadata in `MarkResolved`, and updated unit, functional, and fuzz test coverage.",
  "card": "Extends getprivatebroadcastinfo to retain and report finished private broadcast transactions rather than dropping them immediately upon network receipt. Adds a received_by_us field indicating the peer address and timestamp when a transaction is echoed back from the network, and evicts oldest finished transactions when the queue is full. Solves the lack of post-broadcast observability for operators using private transaction broadcast. No dependencies.",
